  "title": "How to Use BIDMAS (Order of Operations)",
  "content": "<p><strong>BIDMAS</strong> tells you the order in which to carry out operations: <strong>B</strong>rackets, <strong>I</strong>ndices (powers and roots), <strong>D</strong>ivision and <strong>M</strong>ultiplication (left to right), <strong>A</strong>ddition and <strong>S</strong>ubtraction (left to right).</p><p>Division and multiplication have <em>equal priority</em> — work left to right. The same applies to addition and subtraction. A common mistake is always doing multiplication before division — this is wrong; you go left to right within each tier.</p><p>When a calculation includes brackets, evaluate everything inside them first. Nested brackets are evaluated from the innermost pair outward. The fraction bar acts like a bracket — evaluate the numerator and denominator separately before dividing.</p><p>For written methods: addition and subtraction use column methods (line up place values); multiplication uses long or grid multiplication; division uses the bus-stop (short division) or long division method.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Calculate</strong> \\(5 + 3 \\times 2^2 - (10 \\div 5)\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Brackets:</strong> \\(10 \\div 5 = 2\\), so the expression becomes \\(5 + 3 \\times 2^2 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Indices:</strong> \\(2^2 = 4\\), giving \\(5 + 3 \\times 4 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Multiplication:</strong> \\(3 \\times 4 = 12\\), giving \\(5 + 12 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4 — Addition and Subtraction (left to right):</strong> \\(5 + 12 = 17\\), then \\(17 - 2 = 15\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(15\\)</p>"
 },
